# repv-env.example - template for the --env file.
#
# Copy to eg /root/.couchdb-prod-env and chmod 600 it. The scripts REFUSE to
# read a credentials file that is group- or other-readable.
#
#     cp repv-env.example /root/.couchdb-prod-env
#     chmod 600 /root/.couchdb-prod-env
#
# One <host>_USER and one <host>_PASS per CouchDB server. The <host> part is the
# hostname or IP without the port, exactly as it appears in the servers table
# (repv-servers.csv) - or, for an older inventory that spells its URLs out in
# full, in that file's ${...} tokens.
#
# Nothing here names an alias. An inventory row saying 'bak1' is resolved to a
# URL by the servers table, and the credentials are then looked up by the
# hostname that URL contains. Renaming an alias therefore never touches this
# file; moving a server to a new IP does.
#
# If two CouchDBs share one box on different ports, a port-qualified key wins
# over the bare hostname:
#
#     192.168.66.9:6984_USER=admin
#     192.168.66.9:6984_PASS=CHANGE_ME
#
# Almost every fleet only ever needs the bare hostname form below.
#
# These keys contain dots, so they are NOT valid shell variable names: this file
# is parsed by the scripts, never `source`d, and never passed through eval.
#
# Values are taken verbatim after an optional single layer of matching quotes,
# so a password may contain = # $ { } and spaces. Do not add a trailing comment
# on a value line - it would become part of the password.

couchdb.example.com_USER=admin
couchdb.example.com_PASS=CHANGE_ME

203.0.113.10_USER=admin
203.0.113.10_PASS=CHANGE_ME

192.168.66.9_USER=admin
192.168.66.9_PASS=CHANGE_ME
